    20	#    an edge source is processed only if the whole translated stencil stays in bounds.
    21	#    Example: suppose a learned relative stencil is {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)}. A top-edge
    22	#    source at (0, 3) is valid and paints those three cells in the target color. A top
    23	#    edge source at (0, 4) is invalid because (0, 5) would leave the grid, so the source
    24	#    simply disappears in the output instead of producing a clipped fragment.
    25	# b) Complexity calibration:
    26	#    The generator keeps the original 1-4 rules, unique source->target color pairs, and
    27	#    small 8-connected teal stencils, but now every scattered source lies on the border.
    28	#    Each rule is forced to have both valid edge sources and invalid edge sources so the
    29	#    learner must combine edge detection with boundary-fit checking rather than seeing a
    30	#    degenerate all-valid or all-invalid sample.
    31	# c) Audit of original assumptions:
    32	#    - The original `main()` treated every non-legend source-colored pixel as actionable;
    33	#      that is no longer correct because interior source-colored pixels should not trigger
    34	#      stamping in this variant. The solver now explicitly filters to border cells.
    35	#    - The original `ok = ...` clipping write assumed partial stencils were legal. That
    36	#      assumption is broken by D15/0, so the solver now requires the full translated
    37	#      stencil to fit or skips the source entirely.
    38	#    - Preserving non-source bystanders still works because the generator never reuses a
    39	#      learned source color for bystanders, and bystanders are kept away from the border
    40	#      for clarity.
    41	# d) Placement legality:
    42	#    Legends are placed strictly in the interior so the "edge sources" cue is clean.
    43	#    Scattered source pixels are placed only on border cells, remain 4-neighbor isolated,
    44	#    and never overlap legends or each other. Valid stamps are also kept away from other
    45	#    valid output footprints and previously placed source pixels so the output stays legible.
    46	# e) Visual observability:
    47	#    Across samples, the border contains the actionable source pixels while the legend sits
    48	#    inside the board. Some edge sources generate the full target-colored stencil and some
    49	#    vanish, making the fit-vs-overflow distinction visible from the geometry alone.
    50	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    51	#    Legend regions, edge sources, valid output stamps, and bystanders are all separated by
    52	#    reservation. Bystanders are interior-only and avoid output footprints so preserved
    53	#    pixels do not masquerade as failed edge sources.
    54	# g) Color inference:
    55	#    Colors still follow the base task: the adjacent legend pair teaches which source color
    56	#    maps to which target color, and the nearby teal component is the stencil. The new
    57	#    decision point is purely spatial: actionable instances are on the border, and only
    58	#    those whose full stencil fits are realized.
    59	# h) Variant metric used in testing:
    60	#    Count border source pixels whose translated stencil is invalid. Samples should have a
    61	#    positive invalid count and should differ from a clipped-stamping baseline on many
    62	#    generated boards; otherwise D15/0 has visually degenerated.
    63	# i) New solver assumptions and generator guarantees:
    64	#    - The solver assumes non-legend actionable instances are border cells only.
    65	#      The generator therefore places every scattered source on the outer frame.
    66	#    - The solver assumes a skipped source leaves no residue at all.
    67	#      The generator builds outputs from bystanders plus valid edge stamps only.
    68	#    - The solver assumes legends are discoverable without interference from scattered
    69	#      pixels. The generator enforces 4-neighbor isolation and interior legend placement.
